.TH INSTALLE 1 "2026-07-31" "senechal" "bashified verb"
.SH NAME
installe \- govern what is reachable from a prompt
.SH SYNOPSIS
.\" bashify: norun
.B installe
\fIpath\fR
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B installe verb
\fIproject\fR \fIname\fR
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B installe retire
\fIname\fR
.br
.B installe list
.br
.B installe audit
.br
.B installe
[\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-f\fR|\fB\-\-force\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-\-version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B installe
governs one directory \-
.I ~/.local/bin
\- and one question about it: which utilities a prompt can reach by name.
Adding and removing are the same act in two directions, so they are one verb.
A utility that can only add is how an install directory becomes a landfill: the
cost of putting something on the path is one command and the cost of taking it
off is an archaeology session, so nothing ever comes off.
.PP
.B installe
therefore refuses to be write\-only. Every install is recorded, so every
install can be undone by name, by a caller who was not there when it happened
and does not know what it pointed at.
.SS Ownership
.B installe
maintains a manifest of what it put on the path, and it removes nothing that
manifest does not name. The install directory already holds files
a human placed there by hand, files a scheduler generates, dated backups, and
vendored binaries. Deleting one of those because it looked like debris is not a
cleanup; it is a loss with a tidy commit message in front of it.
.PP
So
.B retire
refuses an unowned entry and exits
.BR 7 .
The refusal names what it saw and the flag that overrides it. Passing
.B \-\-force
removes it anyway, which is the caller taking the decision back \- the point is
that the decision is taken, not defaulted into.
.SS Declaring the change
What a prompt can reach by name is machine\-wide configuration, so every change
to it is filed with
.BR notify\-senechal ,
by
.B installe
itself, on the caller's behalf. A change a caller has to remember to declare is
a change that goes undeclared, and the record of what this machine can run
stops matching the machine.
.PP
The note is filed after the change succeeds, never before, and names the entry,
the directory, and what it points at. A dry run declares nothing, because it
changed nothing.
.B \-\-quiet
does not suppress it: quiet governs commentary to the caller, not the
obligation to the estate.
.PP
If
.B notify\-senechal
is absent from the path, that is reported as
.B 8
rather than passed over. The change stands \- it succeeded \- but the estate was
not told, and a missing guard is a finding, not an inconvenience. Setting
.B INSTALLE_NOTIFY=0
declines the filing deliberately, which is a different act from it failing
silently.
.SS The subcommands
.TP
.B (a path)
Make one executable reachable by its own basename, by symlink. The target must
exist, be a regular file, and be executable; the name must be free, or the
existing entry must already be one
.B installe
owns and points elsewhere \- in which case it is repointed and said so.
.TP
.B verb
Make a bashified verb reachable. The verb is read from
.IR project 's
.B bashified
branch, which is checked out as a
.BR git\-worktree (1)
beside the project if it is not already, and the link points into that
worktree. This is the only form that creates anything outside the install
directory, and it prints the worktree it created.
.TP
.B retire
The negation. Remove one name from the path and from the manifest. What it
removes is the link, never the target: a retired verb is unreachable, not
deleted, and re\-installing it costs one command.
.TP
.B list
What
.B installe
owns, as
.IR name , TAB , target .
This is the population
.B retire
acts on.
.TP
.B audit
Classify every entry in the install directory by where it came from. Read\-only.
Provenance is the fact a cleanup needs and the fact a directory listing does not
carry. Seven classes:
.RS
.TP
.B owned
.B installe
put it there; it is in the manifest, and
.B retire
removes it without argument.
.TP
.B repo\-link
A symlink into a checkout. Retiring it unwires a project; the project is
untouched.
.TP
.B link
A symlink pointing somewhere else \- a language runtime, a vendored tree.
.TP
.B generated
A generator wrote it, either by a naming convention or by its own
"generated by" header. Retire it at the generator, not here: rerunning the
generator puts it straight back.
.TP
.B backup
A dated or suffixed copy of something else.
.TP
.B unknown
A plain file with nothing declaring where it came from. A human put it here.
.B installe
does not guess.
.TP
.B broken
The target is gone. The one class that is always safe to retire.
.RE
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print the change; do not make it. Every writing form honours this.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Remove an entry
.B installe
does not own. Refused by default; see
.B Ownership
above.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine\-readable output.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Results on standard output, commentary suppressed.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Usage summary, including the exit vocabulary.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the utility name and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-
End of flags; everything after is a positional argument.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
kept \- the path now says what the caller asked it to say.
.TP
.B 2
usage error \- unknown subcommand, or a required argument missing.
.TP
.B 5
broken \- the change was attempted and failed. The install directory is left as
it was found.
.TP
.B 6
blind \- the install directory could not be read, so its contents cannot be
reported or changed.
.TP
.B 7
refused \-
.B retire
was asked to remove an entry
.B installe
does not own. Project\-specific, and deliberately not folded into
.BR 5 :
nothing broke. A guard held.
.TP
.B 8
undeclared \- the path was changed and
.B notify\-senechal
could not file it. Project\-specific. The change stands; what failed is the
declaration of it, and the two are reported separately so neither can be
mistaken for the other.
.TP
.B 9
nothing matched \-
.B list
or
.B audit
found nothing, or
.B retire
was given a name that is not on the path. Not an error, and project\-specific:
the shell convention for this is
.BR 1 ,
but the ecosystem reserves every code through
.B 6
for the shared vocabulary, so a verb that wants a seventh meaning takes it
above the reserved range rather than redefining a code every other verb
already uses.
.PP
.B installe
cannot spend money. It carries no
.B \-\-summon
flag and makes no network request.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/bin
The install directory it governs. Overridden by
.BR INSTALLE_BIN .
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/installe/manifest.tsv
The record of what it owns, one
.IR name , TAB , target , TAB , date
per line. Removing this file does not remove anything from the path; it makes
.B installe
disown it, and
.B audit
reports it as unknown provenance thereafter.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B INSTALLE_BIN
The install directory. Defaults to
.IR ~/.local/bin .
.TP
.B INSTALLE_MANIFEST
The ownership record. Defaults to
.IR ~/.local/share/installe/manifest.tsv .
.TP
.B INSTALLE_NOTIFY
Set to
.B 0
to decline filing the change with
.BR notify\-senechal .
Anything else, or unset, files it.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Asking for a name this directory does not install:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ installe retire no\-such\-utility
installe: no\-such\-utility is not on the path
$ echo $?
9
.fi
.RE
.PP
Every writing form takes
.BR \-n ,
and a dry run reports nothing it did not do:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ installe \-n retire no\-such\-utility
installe: no\-such\-utility is not on the path
$ echo $?
9
.fi
.RE
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ installe \-\-version
installe (bashified)
.fi
.RE
.PP
The other forms report the state of one particular machine, so an expected
output here is a fixture rather than a fact, so none is written:
.B installe audit
classifies every entry in the install directory,
.B installe list
prints what
.B installe
owns,
.B installe retire
on an entry it does not own is refused with
.BR 7 ,
and
.B installe verb senechal recense
makes one bashified verb reachable.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR recense (1),
.BR ln (1),
.BR update\-alternatives (1),
.BR stow (1),
.BR notify\-senechal (1)
.PP
.B recense
is the instrument that checks
.BR installe :
it reports what the path actually reaches, from the outside, without consulting
the manifest. An install
.B installe
believes it made and
.B recense
cannot see is a broken install, and the disagreement is the test.
